[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Error Handling & Custom Extensions in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL handles errors differently from traditional REST APIs. While REST relies heavily on HTTP status codes (like 400, 404, or 500) to communicate failures, GraphQL typically returns a 200 OK status code even when errors occur, embedding the error details directly in the response payload. Mastering error handling and custom extensions is critical for building robust, enterprise-ready GraphQL services."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Anatomy of a GraphQL Error"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When an operation fails partially or completely, the GraphQL response contains both a "data" and an "errors" field. According to the specification, each error object can contain a message, locations, path, and an optional "extensions" dictionary for metadata."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"{
  "data": {
    "user": null
  },
  "errors": [
    {
      "message": "User not found",
      "locations": [{ "line": 2, "column": 3 }],
      "path": ["user"],
      "extensions": {
        "code": "USER_NOT_FOUND",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-25T14:30:00Z"
      }
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Custom Extensions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The "extensions" field is your primary mechanism for attaching domain-specific metadata to errors. This is commonly used for error codes, localization keys, validation fields, or debugging metrics. Below is an example of implementing a custom GraphQLError in Node.js using Apollo Server."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import { GraphQLError } from 'graphql';

const throwDetailedError = () => {
  throw new GraphQLError('Access denied to sensitive resource', {
    extensions: {
      code: 'FORBIDDEN',
      http: {
        status: 403
      },
      requiredRole: 'ADMIN'
    }
  });
};"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Production Error Handling"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sanitize production errors: Never leak internal database errors, stack traces, or raw SQL queries to client applications."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use standardized error codes: Implement a consistent naming convention inside extensions.code (e.g., UNAUTHENTICATED, BAD_USER_INPUT, INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage formatting formatError hooks: Centralize error logging and formatting in your server configuration rather than catching them manually in every resolver."}]
